**GROUP THREE - TASK III (10 Marks)**

The **Body Mass Index (BMI)** is a value derived from the mass (weight) and height of an individual, male or female.

Note the following:

- BMI = (mass or weight)/(height*height) expressed in units of Kg/m$^2$,
- height is in meters
- mass or weight is in Kg

**C program**

```c
#include<stdio.h>
int main() {
  float w1, h1, bmi1, w2, h2, bmi2;
  printf("Enter Mary's height in metres: ");
  scanf("%f", &h1);
  printf("Enter Mary's weight in kilograms: ");
  scanf("%f", &w1);
  printf("Enter John's height in centimetres: ");
  scanf("%f", &h2);
  printf("Enter John's weight in grams: ");
  scanf("%f", &w2);
  bmi1 = w1/(h1*h1);
  bmi2 = (w2/1000)/(h2/100 * h2/100);
  printf("\nBody Mass Index\n");
  printf("Mary: %.2f\n", bmi1);
  printf("John: %.2f\n", bmi2);
  getchar();
}
```

**Pascal program**

```pascal
Program BMI;
Var
  w1, h1, bmi1, w2, h2, bmi2 : real;
Begin
  write('Enter Mary''s height in metres: ');
  readln(h1);
  write ('Enter Mary''s weight in kilograms: ');
  readln(w1);
  write ('Enter John''s height in centimetres: ');
  readln(h2);
  write ('Enter John''s weight in grams: ');
  readln(w2);
  bmi1 := w1/(h1*h1);
  bmi2 := (w2/1000)/(h2/100 * h2/100);
  writeln;
  writeln ('Body Mass Index');
  writeln ('Mary: ', bmi1:0:2);
  write ('John: ', bmi2:0:2);
  readln();
End.
```
